bibliograph (0.5.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The description backfill is scheduled: a daily timer enqueues one bounded
    batch (`--limit 20000`, ~20% of the shared Google Books quota) of
    `backfill-edition-description` jobs that the existing worker drains. The
    scan gained `--dry-run`/`--limit` and releases its pool, and rows it defers
    stay actionable for the next tick.
  * A 429 is no longer recorded as a permanent miss. `description_checked_at`
    is only stamped when no upstream request failed during the lookup, decided
    by a new lifetime-failure counter on the circuit breaker, so rows skipped
    during a quota-exhausted day are retried instead of being lost.
  * Contributor stubs no longer enqueue a publish job. The 0.4.0 OpenLibrary
    removal deleted the `ingest-contributor` task, so every minted stub queued
    a job no handler would ever pick up — one catalog pass left 21,303 inert
    rows — and the per-name insert dominated the walk. The stub row is upserted
    synchronously, which is what serving and matching read; ingest persists
    records in Postgres, exactly as edition ingest already does.

