bibliograph (0.2.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The Goodreads metadata backfill stops when the failure rate reaches 20%
    of attempted rows (not-found plus hard failures, after at least five
    attempts) and records the stop reason in its summary. A Goodreads
    anti-bot response still stops the run immediately.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 13:10:00 -0500

bibliograph (0.2.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Goodreads is now a general-search source: title/author results are
    synthesized as `gr.<bookId>` editions, contributors are resolved by name,
    and provider blocks degrade the response instead of failing it.
  * Edition metadata backfill from Goodreads fills format, pages, ISBN/ASIN,
    publication year, and language; it logs `goodreads-book-not-found`,
    counts blocked responses separately, and stops on an anti-bot response.
  * The Goodreads scraper extracts genres, ISBN/ASIN, publication date,
    publisher, language, and contributor portraits; author pages are the
    portrait fallback.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500

bibliograph (0.2.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Cover bytes from Google Books and Open Library are cached on disk. The
    `getImageForBook` response now points at `/image/cover`, which fetches an
    allowlisted provider URL once, writes it under `COVER_CACHE_DIR`, and
    serves later readers from disk. Concurrent misses share one upstream
    request; redirects are followed only within the provider allowlist.
  * The Debian unit provisions `/var/lib/bibliograph/covers` through
    `StateDirectory=` so the cache survives upgrades, and the conffile documents
    the path.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 00:05:00 -0500

bibliograph (0.2.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The `net.olamaelcu.livtet.biblio.graph.follow` procedure is gone, along with
    the `follows` table it wrote. Its purpose was to keep a local copy of the
    follow graph for the social feeds, but nothing read it: 0.2.5 made those
    feeds read `net.olamaelcu.livtet.biblio.follow` records from the reader's
    own repo. Following and unfollowing are now purely a PDS write from the
    client; there is no Bibliograph-side index to keep in step.
  * `0022_drop_follows` drops the table. Combined with `0021_follows`, which
    created it, the pair is idempotent: an environment that never applied either
    still ends up without the table.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Wed, 04 Nov 2026 13:30:00 -0500

